<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi was a Zaydi religious leader and former member of the Yemeni parliament who founded the movement that bears his family's name. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Saada</a> region he preached against Western and Saudi influence and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a>. He was killed by government forces in September 2004 in the first of six wars, and his brothers led the movement after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi.jpg" alt="Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elnur777536276 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hussein_housi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hussein al-Houthi was born on 20 August 1959 in the Marran area of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, the ancestral home of the Houthi tribe; some sources give 1956 as the year. His father, Badreddin al-Houthi, was a prominent Zaydi cleric. According to a disciple, he lived for part of his life in Qom, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with his family, including his father and his younger brother Abdul-Malik; the same disciple claimed that he was close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-khamenei">Ali Khamenei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-leader-of-iran">Supreme Leader of Iran</a>, and to Hassan Nasrallah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He sat in the Yemeni parliament from 1993 to 1997 for Al-Haqq, the Party of Truth, a party of Zaydi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> membership. When the party supported South Yemeni separatism it became a target of the government, and he fled, allegedly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. After his return he broke with Al-Haqq. In 1990 or 1992 he founded the Believing Youth movement to teach young people about Zaydi Islam and its history and so to revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaydism">Zaydism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada-governorate">Saada Governorate</a>.</p><p>The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> accused him of trying to set himself up as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, of opening unlicensed religious centres, of creating an armed group called Ansarallah and of staging violent protests against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Israel. His followers held that the government was too closely allied with the United States.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 June 2004 police arrested 640 of his followers who were demonstrating in front of the Great Mosque of Sanaa, and two days later the government offered a bounty of 55,000 US dollars for his capture and began an operation against him. In July the army killed 25 of his supporters and raised the bounty to 75,500 dollars. On 10 September the interior and defence ministries announced that he had been killed with 20 of his aides in Marran.</p><p>His father briefly took control of the movement after his death. The government buried him at the central prison of Sanaa so that his grave would not become a shrine. After Saleh stepped down, the new government handed his remains to his family on 28 December 2012, and on 5 June 2013 tens of thousands attended his reburial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Saada</a>. His brothers Abdul-Malik, Yahia and Abdul-Karim are leaders of the movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hussein al-Houthi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the north the government fought six wars between 2004 and 2010 against the Houthi movement, a Zaydi revivalist group whose founder <b>Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi</b> was killed in 2004; Saudi Arabia joined the fighting in 2009.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ar Ruways, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada-governorate">Saada Governorate</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.26,43.28&amp;year=1994" aria-label="Ar Ruways on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5726 1523 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6176 1793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6176 1793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.26,43.28&amp;year=1994">What was here in 1994? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
